# Dogri in Devanagari. espeak-ng has no Dogri voice: compatible mode is the
# Hindi reading (as with the reference's Hindi voice); this file is the
# improved reading. Written for uccharan from these descriptions:
#   Ved Kumari Ghai (1991), Studies in Phonetics and Phonology, with special
#     reference to Dogri; and her account of Dogri tone (voiced aspirates
#     and h lose voice or aspiration and leave a tone);
#   Ujjal Singh Bahri (2001), Dogri: Phonology and Grammatical Sketch;
#   Colin Masica (1991), The Indo-Aryan Languages, on tone in Punjabi and
#   Dogri.
# Tone is written as in uccharan's Punjabi: a grave accent for low tone, an
# acute accent for high tone, on the vowel that carries it.
#   - A word-initial voiced aspirate (घ झ ढ ध भ) is said voiceless and
#     unaspirated, and the vowel after it has low tone: घर kʌ̀ɾ.
#   - An h before a vowel (word-initial ह, or ह after a consonant: म्हीना,
#     ज्हार) is not said, and that vowel has low tone: ज्हार ɟàːɾ.
#   - A voiced aspirate inside or at the end of a word loses its aspiration
#     and stays voiced; an h after a vowel (ह् as Dogri writes it, the
#     apostrophe of र’लाया, the avagraha of लाऽ) is not said. A stressed
#     vowel before either has high tone (साह्ब sáːb, त्रीह् tɾíː);
#     otherwise the vowel after it has low tone.
# Inherent vowel deletion is the Hindi type; ऐ औ are ɛː ɔː; ष is ʃ, क़ ख़ ग़
# are k kʰ ɡ (Bahri: q x ɣ are not Dogri sounds).

